 at least one per diagonal — at which diagonal k+1 is black, or else a certificate that diagonal k+1 is white from N k + 1 on for good. Then diagonal k has already settled by N k. The black branch is leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_of_black, which resets the next diagonal at that black cell with the period unchanged; the white branch is the first arm of leftDiagonal_step_onset_dichotomy, which costs one index and doubles the period, and the second arm is impossible because it produces a black cell past N k + 1. leftDiagonal_onset_le then follows from a ladder with N k ≤ k. This is what crystal 45 calls `iterating it bounds the onset by the sum of the first-black gaps`, stated as a theorem instead of as a remark, and it is a genuine advance on the closed leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_line: that node's onset is pinned to the line N k = k and its second disjunct is a return condition, where this takes an arbitrary ladder and a whiteness condition — the same eventually-white object leftDiagonal_period_le_of_white_count counts. The route below compiles, exit 0, no sorry: explorer/seed_scratch_ladder.lean. Measured (explorer/seed_gapgrowth.mjs): the greedy ladder that always takes the next black cell reaches N k / k = 2.000 by k = 2000, so this reduction cannot on its own beat onset ≤ 2k, and the truth is about 0.34 k.",","-    "disclaims": "Says nothing about whether such a ladder with N k ≤ k exists — that is the wall. Do not read the measured N k / k → 2 as a proof of anything: the greedy ladder cannot retreat and the real seam does, on about a quarter of rows (crystal 52). Not a prize conjecture; the left edge only.",","+    "id": "leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree",","+    "lean_name": "leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree",","+    "statement": "theorem leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree (k p T : ℕ) (hT : T ≤ 2 * k)\n    (h : rowNat T % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1)) :\n    PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal k) p k := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The onset wall in one congruence per diagonal, and the interface every packed-row attempt on it wants. Diagonal k at index j is bit k of the packed row j + k (leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit, closed), so 'diagonal k has settled by index k' asks exactly that rows T and T + p agree on bit k for every T ≥ 2k. Agreement on the low k+1 bits at one time T ≤ 2k is enough, because the low bits are autonomous — see rowNat_agree_forward in this tier — and bit k depends on nothing above the residue mod 2^(k+1). Why a proof of the wall cites it: the wall follows in three lines by choosing T and p per k, checked as an `example` in explorer/seeder_scratch_front.lean, and this form is strictly freer than either closed reduction. leftDiagonal_onset_le_iff_rowNat_return pins T = 2k and p = 2^k; leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_stepMod_preperiod demands the preperiod bound for EVERY start x < 2^(k+1) when only the orbit of 1 is the seed's row. Here T and p are the prover's to choose. It is also the shape leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_le_5000 actually used to reach depth 5000 on the single shift 16, stated once so the next attempt does not rebuild it a fifth time.",","+    "disclaims": "Says nothing about whether such a congruence exists at large k; that is the wall itself, and what remains after this node is a lower bound on where the width-(k+1) truncated orbit of 1 first returns. The shift p must be allowed to vary with k and this statement does: no single p serves every k, because leftDiagonal_period_unbounded is proved and crystal 57 gives each uniform constant's exact expiry (16 dies at k = 87867). Not a prize conjecture; the left edge only.",","     "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have d0 : ∀ j, leftDiagonal 0 j = true := by\n    intro j; simpa [leftDiagonal] using evolve_left_edge j\n  have d1 : ∀ j, leftDiagonal 1 j = true := by\n    intro j; exact evolve_left_second_diagonal j\n  have weaken : ∀ (f : ℕ → Bool) (p a b : ℕ), a ≤ b → PeriodicFrom f p a → PeriodicFrom f p b := by\n    intro f p a b hab h n hn; exact h n (le_trans hab hn)\n  have key : ∀ k, ∃ p, 0 < p ∧ PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal k) p (N k) ∧\n      PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (k + 1)) p (N k) := by\n    intro k\n    induction k with\n    | zero =>\n      exact ⟨1, one_pos, fun n _ => by rw [d0, d0], fun n _ => by rw [d1, d1]⟩\n    | succ k ih =>\n      obtain ⟨p, hp, ha, hb⟩ := ih\n      have hlt := hmono k\n      rcases hwitness k with hblack | hwhite\n      · obtain ⟨j, hj⟩ : ∃ j, N (k + 1) = j + 1 ∧ N k ≤ j :=\n          ⟨N (k + 1) - 1, by omega, by omega⟩\n        rw [hj.1] at hblack ⊢\n        have hstep := leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_of_black k p (N k) j hj.2 ha hb hblack\n        exact ⟨p, hp, weaken _ _ _ _ (by omega) hb, hstep⟩\n      · rcases leftDiagonal_step_onset_dichotomy k p (N k) ha hb with h | ⟨j, hj, hjb, -⟩\n        · refine ⟨2 * p, by omega, ?_, ?_⟩\n          · exact weaken _ _ _ _ (le_of_lt hlt) (periodicFrom_mul _ p (N k) hb 2)\n          · exact weaken _ _ _ _ (by omega) h\n        · exact absurd hjb (by rw [hwhite (j + 1) (by omega)]; exact Bool.noConfusion)\n  obtain ⟨p, hp, ha, -⟩ := key k\n  exact ⟨p, hp, ha⟩",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.EvolveLeftEdge", "Rule30.Proofs.EvolveLeftSecondDiagonal", "Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalPeriodicFromStepOfBlack", "Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalStepOnsetDichotomy", "Rule30.Proofs.PeriodicFromMul"]","+      "tactics": "  have fwd : ∀ t, T ≤ t → rowNat t % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (t + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1) := by\n    intro t hTt\n    obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le hTt\n    subst hd\n    simp only [rowNat_mod_eq_iterate] at h ⊢\n    have e1 : T + d = d + T := by omega\n    have e2 : T + d + p = d + (T + p) := by omega\n    rw [e2, e1, Function.iterate_add_apply, Function.iterate_add_apply, h]\n  intro n hn\n  rw [leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit, leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit]\n  have hag := fwd (n + k) (by omega)\n  have key : ∀ m : ℕ, (rowNat m).testBit k = (rowNat m % 2 ^ (k + 1)).testBit k := by\n    intro m; rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]; simp\n  rw [show n + p + k = n + k + p from by omega, key (n + k + p), key (n + k), hag]",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.RowNatModEqIterate", "Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalEqRowNatTestBit"]","+    },","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 7).all fun k => (List.range (2 * k + 1)).all fun T => (List.range 9).all fun p => !(decide (rowNat T % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1))) || (List.range 12).all fun m => decide ((rowNat (2 * k + m + p)).testBit k = (rowNat (2 * k + m)).testBit k)",","+      "range": "k < 7, T ≤ 2k, p < 9, conclusion read at 12 successive indices — entirely in the packed row model, so no 3^t cost and no depth wall"","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "leftDiagonal_not_both_eventually_white",","-    "lean_name": "leftDiagonal_not_both_eventually_white",","-    "statement": "theorem leftDiagonal_not_both_eventually_white (k : ℕ) :\n    ¬ ((∃ N, ∀ j ≥ N, leftDiagonal k j = false) ∧\n        ∃ M, ∀ j ≥ M, leftDiagonal (k + 1) j = false) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The first structural constraint on the eventually-white diagonals — the object crystals 57 to 60 report four independent routes converging on, and which no node on the board states. No two of them are adjacent. The proof descends: leftDiagonal_shift_of_white says that past a white diagonal m+2 the diagonal m read two cells along equals diagonal m+1 read one cell along, so if m+1 is white too then m is white; the pair (m, m+1) is then in the same position one diagonal further out, and the descent ends at diagonals 0 and 1, which are constantly black (evolve_left_edge, evolve_left_second_diagonal). A proof of leftDiagonal_period_le has to bound the number of eventually-white diagonals below k, and this is the only constraint on that set anyone here has proved: it improves the trivial count k to k/2, which is nowhere near the log2 (k+1) the wall needs, but it is the first fact about the set rather than about one of its members. It is also what stops leftDiagonal_period_le_of_white_count from being read as permitting a white diagonal at every index. Verified below 400, where the eventually-white diagonals are 2, 7, 28 and 399 (explorer/seed_whitegaps.mjs). The route below compiles, exit 0, no sorry: explorer/seed_scratch_routes.lean.",","-    "disclaims": "k/2 is not a useful bound and this does not pretend to be one. Not a prize conjecture; the left edge only.",","-    "under": "leftDiagonal_period_le",","+    "id": "rowNat_agree_forward",","+    "lean_name": "rowNat_agree_forward",","+    "statement": "theorem rowNat_agree_forward (n T p t : ℕ) (hTt : T ≤ t)\n    (h : rowNat T % 2 ^ n = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ n) :\n    rowNat t % 2 ^ n = rowNat (t + p) % 2 ^ n := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The low bits of a row are autonomous, so an agreement between two rows can never be lost. Bit b of row t+1 reads only bits b, b-1, b-2 of row t, so truncating to the low n bits commutes with the step; two rows agreeing there agree there for ever after. Three lines from the closed rowNat_mod_eq_iterate: the truncated rows are the orbit of 1 under stepMod n, so if two orbit points coincide at T, applying the map t − T more times keeps them coincident. Harvest rather than invention, and this is the argument for the node: it has now been re-proved privately three times, in three different sessions, under three names — the 'one autonomy induction' inside leftDiagonal_onset_le_iff_rowNat_return, `iterate_eq_of_eq` inside leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_le_5000, and part of `agree_bdry` inside the walk parked by onset attempts 11 and 12. It is the same case periodicFrom_trans_period was seeded on. Any proof of leftDiagonal_onset_le through the packed row cites it, because it is the only reason an agreement front is monotone in time, and monotone is what lets a front reach height k+1 by row 2k.",","+    "disclaims": "Pure bookkeeping about a deterministic map on finitely many states; there is no automaton content and no bound on anything. It says agreement is never lost, not that it is ever gained — gaining a bit is rowNat_return_succ_iff, already closed, and how often that succeeds is the wall.",","+    "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  induction k with\n  | zero =>\n    rintro ⟨⟨N, hN⟩, -⟩\n    have := hN N le_rfl\n    have hb : leftDiagonal 0 N = true := evolve_left_edge N\n    rw [hb] at this\n    exact Bool.noConfusion this\n  | succ m ih =>\n    rintro ⟨⟨N, hN⟩, ⟨M, hM⟩⟩\n    have hshift := leftDiagonal_shift_of_white m M (fun i hi => hM i hi)\n    refine ih ⟨⟨max N M + 2, ?_⟩, ⟨N, hN⟩⟩\n    intro j hj\n    have h1 : leftDiagonal m ((j - 2) + 2) = leftDiagonal (m + 1) ((j - 2) + 1) :=\n      hshift (j - 2) (by omega)\n    have h2 : leftDiagonal (m + 1) ((j - 2) + 1) = false := hN _ (by omega)\n    rw [show j = (j - 2) + 2 by omega, h1, h2]",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.EvolveLeftEdge", "Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalShiftOfWhite"]","+      "tactics": "  obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le hTt\n  subst hd\n  simp only [rowNat_mod_eq_iterate] at h ⊢\n  have e1 : T + d = d + T := by omega\n  have e2 : T + d + p = d + (T + p) := by omega\n  rw [e2, e1, Function.iterate_add_apply, Function.iterate_add_apply, h]",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.RowNatModEqIterate"]","+    },","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 30).all fun T => (List.range 6).all fun n => (List.range 6).all fun p => (List.range 10).all fun d => !(decide (rowNat T % 2 ^ n = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ n)) || decide (rowNat (T + d) % 2 ^ n = rowNat (T + d + p) % 2 ^ n)",","+      "range": "T < 30, n < 6, p < 6, d < 10 — 10,800 instances of the implication, in the packed row model, so no 3^t cost"","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "periodicFrom_trans_period",","-    "lean_name": "periodicFrom_trans_period",","-    "statement": "theorem periodicFrom_trans_period (f : ℕ → Bool) (p q N M : ℕ) (hp : 0 < p)\n    (hN : PeriodicFrom f p N) (hM : PeriodicFrom f q M) : PeriodicFrom f q N := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "A machine, harvested rather than invented: a sequence that already has one positive period from N inherits every other of its periods from N too, whatever onset that other period came with. It has now been re-proved privately inside three separate pieces of work — leftDiagonal_onset_le_iff_rowNat_return's proof note names it as the step its forward direction needs, and the parked file of the eleventh attempt on the onset wall defines it again with the comment that it is private in two closed files. Every argument that walks a ladder of diagonals has to put two periodicity facts with different onsets and different periods on a common footing, and this is the one lemma that does it; both leftDiagonal_period_le_of_white_count and leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_black_ladder above want it the moment their two branches are merged. The proof is one calc: hop forward by M periods of length p to get past M, apply the q-period there, hop back. The route below compiles, exit 0, no sorry: explorer/seed_scratch_routes.lean.",","-    "disclaims": "Pure sequence bookkeeping; there is no automaton in it and it proves nothing about rule 30.",","+    "id": "rowNat_testBit_zero",","+    "lean_name": "rowNat_testBit_zero",","+    "statement": "theorem rowNat_testBit_zero (t : ℕ) : (rowNat t).testBit 0 = true := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The left edge of the cone, said in the packed row model: the lowest bit of every row is set, so every row is an odd number. leftDiagonal 0 is the left edge and is black at every index (evolve_left_edge, closed), and leftDiagonal 0 t is bit 0 of row t (leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit, closed), so this is those two composed and nothing else. Why an onset proof cites it: it is the base case of every front argument on the packed rows. A front starts at height 1 precisely because any two rows agree on their lowest bit, and the walk parked by attempts 11 and 12 had to prove this privately (as rowNat_testBit_zero, then again as `agree_one`) before it could take its first step. It also records why the halving law does not apply to the seed's own orbit: step_two_mul makes the even states a copy of the whole system one bit narrower, and this says rowNat never enters them.",","+    "disclaims": "Supply, not insight — a change of notation for a fact the board closed long ago, provable in three lines from two closed nodes. It bears on no prize conjecture, and it gives no bound on any onset or period.",","+    "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  intro n hn\n  have hMp : M ≤ M * p := Nat.le_mul_of_pos_right M hp\n  have h1 := periodicFrom_mul f p N hN M (n + q) (by omega)\n  have h2 := periodicFrom_mul f p N hN M n hn\n  have h3 := hM (n + M * p) (by omega)\n  rw [← h1, ← h2, ← h3]\n  congr 1\n  omega",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.PeriodicFromMul"]","-    }","-  },","-  {","-    "id": "centerColumn_eq_rowNat_testBit",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_eq_rowNat_testBit",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_eq_rowNat_testBit (t : ℕ) : centerColumn t = (rowNat t).testBit t := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The missing edge between P1's own object and the row model every recent P1 node runs on. leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit reads a diagonal off one bit of a packed row, and six closed nodes since — rowNat_mod_eq_iterate, leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_le_5000, stepMod_preperiod_le_of_le_11, step_two_mul, stepMod_iterate_two_mul, leftDiagonal_onset_le_iff_stepMod_return — are stated entirely in that model because the kernel can compute row 5000 in a second where evolve gives out near row 18. The centre column has no such edge, so no fact about it can be settled by decide at all. With this it can: read together with rowNat_mod_eq_iterate it is crystal 60's dictionary, centerColumn t is bit t of the t-th iterate of r ↦ 4r XOR (2r OR r) from 1, which states the P1 residual with no automaton in it and makes crystal 19's finite exclusions reachable the way stepMod_preperiod_le_of_le_11 was. Geometrically it is also centerColumn t = leftDiagonal t 0: the centre column is every diagonal read at index 0, where settledCenter reads the same diagonal past its onset — which is why the two agree for t < 18 and part company after. The route below compiles, exit 0, no sorry: explorer/seed_scratch_routes.lean. Checked against OEIS A051023 for t < 41 in explorer/seed_parity.mjs.",","-    "disclaims": "Supply, not insight, and it does not bear on aperiodicity: it is a change of notation for the centre column, and every hard thing about that sequence survives it unchanged. It opens the door to kernel computations about the centre column, and a computed prefix is not a theorem about the limit — no rung of that ladder can close P1.",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have h := rowCell_eq_evolve t 0\n  unfold rowCell at h\n  rw [if_pos (by omega : -(t : ℤ) ≤ (0 : ℤ) ∧ (0 : ℤ) ≤ (t : ℤ))] at h\n  simpa [centerColumn] using h.symm",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.RowCellEqEvolve"]","+      "tactics": "  have h : leftDiagonal 0 t = true := evolve_left_edge t\n  rw [leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit] at h\n  simpa using h",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.EvolveLeftEdge", "Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalEqRowNatTestBit"]","     },","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 12).all (fun t => centerColumn t == (rowNat t).testBit t)",","-      "imports": [],","-      "range": "t < 12; centerColumn reads evolve, so this costs about 3^11 neighbour evaluations at the last term"","+      "expression": "(List.range 300).all fun t => (rowNat t).testBit 0",","+      "range": "t < 300 — packed row model, no 3^t cost"","     }","   }"," ]}"]}],"originalFile":"{"proposals": [
  {
    "id": "leftDiagonal_period_le_of_white_count",
    "lean_name": "leftDiagonal_period_le_of_white_count",
    "statement": "theorem leftDiagonal_period_le_of_white_count (m q N n : ℕ) (hq : 0 < q) (w : ℕ → ℕ)\n    (hw0 : w 0 = 0) (hmono : ∀ i, w i ≤ w (i + 1))\n    (hstep : ∀ i < n, (∀ J, ∃ j ≥ J, leftDiagonal (m + 1 + i) j = true) ∨ w i < w (i + 1))\n    (h0 : PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal m) q N)\n    (h1 : PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 1)) q N) :\n    ∃ M, PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + n)) (2 ^ w n * q) M ∧\n      PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + n + 1)) (2 ^ w n * q) M := by\n  sorry",
    "reason": "The period wall's residual in the form the board's own closed lemmas already reduce it to, and the first node that states the object four independent routes converged on (crystals 55-60). Carry a pair of neighbouring diagonals with a shared period q. One diagonal further in, leftDiagonal_step_period_dichotomy says either the period survives unchanged, or the middle diagonal is white for ever; and in that second case leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step says the period at most doubles. So the period of diagonal m+n is q multiplied by two once per eventually-white diagonal passed, and by nothing else. The counter w is any nondecreasing function that ticks at each of those, so 2 ^ w n * q is the bound. This is the same induction and the same cited lemma as the closed leftDiagonal_period_le_of_black_between, which is exactly the w = 0 case: there the hypothesis forbids white diagonals outright, here they are permitted and paid for. A proof of leftDiagonal_period_le would cite this and then have only the counting claim left — that at most log2 (k+1) of the first k diagonals are eventually white — which is what that wall's own note says remains after the reduction half was split off. Measured on the packed row model, explorer/seed_whitegaps.mjs, k ≤ 400 from 3000 indices: the pair period max(period k, period k+1) is monotone in k, increases exactly at k = 2, 7, 28, 399, and those are exactly the eventually-white diagonals in range; at every k it equals 2 ^ (number of eventually-white diagonals below k+1), so the bound is attained rather than merely respected.",
    "disclaims": "Proves nothing about how many diagonals are eventually white, which is the whole of what is open. With w counting every diagonal it degenerates to the already-proved 2 ^ k. It is a claim about the left edge of the cone and bears on no prize conjecture.",
    "under": "leftDiagonal_period_le"
  },
